===Verb===
{{en-verb}} {{tlb|en|informal}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To (cause to) [[gush]]; to flow or move in a [[rush]].
#: {{ux|en|The tomato sauce was '''splurged''' all over the chips.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1884|author=w:Donald Grant Mitchell|title=Bound Together, A Sheaf of Papers/Norwich, 1659-1859|location=Norwich|text=But the steamboats come in their time ; and I am sure that I address a large crowd of sympathizing auditors, now that I come to speak of the magnificent old "Fanny," spluttering and paddling, and '''splurging''' up to the little wharf under the lea of Peppers Hill, where the pine wood lay piled in fabulous quantities.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author=w:Edith Wharton|title=[[s:The Custom of the Country/Chapter XXXVIII|The Custom of the Country]]|chapter=Chapter XXXVIII|text=She waited a moment, quivering with the expectation of her husband's answer; then, as none came except the silent darkening of his face, she walked to the door and turned round to fling back: "Of course you can do what you like with your own house, and make any arrangements that suit your family, without consulting me; but you needn't think I'm ever going back to live in that stuffy little hole, with Hubert and his wife '''splurging''' round on top of our heads!"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1930|author=w:Robert E. Howard|title=s:Sailors' Grudge|text="And boy," he '''splurged''', "we are filming a peach, a pip and a wow! Is it a knockout? Oh, baby! A prize-fight picture entitled 'The Honor of the Champion,' starring Reginald Van Veer, with Honey Precious for the herowine. Boy, will it pack the theayters!"}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To spend [[lavishly]] or [[extravagantly]], ''especially'' money. {{defdate|from 1911}}
#: {{syn|en|splash out}}
#: {{ux|en|They decided to '''splurge''' on the biggest banana split for dessert.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1912|author=w:Jack London|title=The House of Pride|text=I could see Schultz think, and revive, and '''splurge''' with his bets again.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 6 2023|author=Sam Lansky|title=Person of Year 2023 : Taylor Swift|url=https://time.com/6342806/person-of-the-year-2023-taylor-swift|work={{w|Time (magazine)|Time}}|passage=Fans flew across the country, stayed in hotels, ate meals out, and '''splurged''' on everything from sweatshirts to limited-edition vinyl, with the average Eras attendee reportedly spending nearly $1,300.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To produce an [[extravagant]] or [[ostentatious]] display.

